1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:01,906
Your workout took an hour.

2
00:00:02,056 --> 00:00:03,554
But where did that hour go?

3
00:00:06,420 --> 00:00:09,253
An hour in the gym is not an hour of lifting.

4
00:00:09,403 --> 00:00:12,746
In this made-up example: fifteen minutes lifting.

5
00:00:12,896 --> 00:00:15,404
Twenty-five resting. Eight setting up.

6
00:00:15,554 --> 00:00:16,611
And twelve waiting.

7
00:00:18,420 --> 00:00:20,568
Those are four different problems.

8
00:00:20,718 --> 00:00:23,586
Start with a delay you can actually change.

9
00:00:23,736 --> 00:00:25,732
Like repeated trips for equipment.

10
00:00:27,420 --> 00:00:29,498
A time audit is not a reason

11
00:00:29,648 --> 00:00:31,692
to rush every rest period.

12
00:00:31,842 --> 00:00:34,732
The biggest block is not automatically the problem.

13
00:00:36,420 --> 00:00:39,137
For one session, log the four buckets.

14
00:00:39,287 --> 00:00:40,993
Make one practical change.

15
00:00:41,143 --> 00:00:42,896
Then compare the next session.

16
00:00:43,046 --> 00:00:45,403
Measure the friction before you rewrite the plan.

17
00:00:47,420 --> 00:00:48,906
Know why. Train better.
